the thought pyramid arts center collects  preserves and studies the contemporary  art of africa  dill humphrey is listed among the 50  most influential artists in africa the  junk man as he calls himself is  preoccupied with the explosive growth of  african cities  and i don't just make art i use junk to  cook soup i can use look at the junk of  the people  and  find out their lifestyle  the state of their financial well-being  their  thinking patterns their world views  all in their junk  in their work dill humphrey and his  colleagues recycle and transform old  clothing and other debris found on the  streets of several african cities  they then create sculptures  installations and performances in which  they aim to reflect the disenfranchised  situation of many african people  dill humphrey and his fellow artists  have exhibited more than 20 works in the  art center in abuja  art lovers here can admire old cement  bags  magazines  empty cans  and other waste material  the campaigns we'll do we'll talk about  the environment  how to manage our waste properly so for  me i've seen a place where the waste is  being converted to act something very  meaningful it makes a whole lot of sense  to the cost of sustainability makes a  whole lot of sense at the cost of  climate change because of managing the  environment  most of the pieces shown at the art  center in abuja are the result of a  two-week training period coordinated by  dill humphrey  the junk man of africa works as a  sculptor performance artist and painter  at the reception he taught people about  the importance of the environment and  waste management when you want to see  the truth  you go to the ugly side of human beings  life itself  is within  themselves they are drunk used and  disused  some of the other artists who had their  artworks on display explain the concept  behind their pieces and the skills  they're taking beyond these walls  we're trying to  make a second meaning  of these wastes  instead of turning them around  we can use them to create something  something very important  something that'll have a permanent life  contemporary african art is increasingly  attracting the attention of global  investors and becoming ever more  valuable for the junk man of africa and  his colleagues it's an opportunity to  embed environmental protection deeper  into the conscience of society
 

